id: rule-consequence-stronger
title: Stronger precondition
topic: Consequence
example-of: CONSEQUENCE
derived: true

task:
  Use the consequence rule to derive this triple. We take $Q'$ equal to
  $Q$ and compute $P'$ with the rule ASSIGN.

pre: x > 120
post: x > 101

scheme: CONSEQUENCE
  First premise: «P» ==> «P'»
  Middle premise: {«P'»} x := x + 1 {«Q'»}
  Last premise: «Q'» ==> «Q»
  ---
  {«P»} x := x + 1 {«Q»}

program:
  {P}
  {P'}
  x := x + 1     // i1
  {Q'}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: Q' and the substitution
part: Middle premise
rule: ASSIGN
focus: i1
text:
  We take $Q' = Q$. Then $P'$ is obtained by substituting the variable $x$
  with the assignment expression $x + 1$ in $Q'$.
set: Q' = x > 101
calc:
  wp(x := x + 1, Q')
  = (x > 101)[x <- x + 1]     // Q' is x > 101
  = x + 1 > 101               // x replaced by x + 1
set: P' = x + 1 > 101

step: Simplify P'
part: Middle premise
rule: LOGIC
focus: P'
text:
  We subtract 1 from both sides. The rule ASSIGN gives the middle premise
  with this $P'$.
calc:
  x + 1 > 101
  = x > 100                   // subtract 1 from both sides
set: P' = x > 100

step: P implies P'
part: First premise
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, P'
text:
  >>> «P» ==> «P'»
vc: x > 120 ==> x > 100
why:
  Every integer greater than 120 is greater than 100.

step: Q' implies Q
part: Last premise
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: Q', Q
text:
  >>> «Q'» ==> «Q»
vc: x > 101 ==> x > 101
why:
  The two formulas are the same. All three premises hold.
